          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong skewfield
            (also spelled skew-field), or
            %strong division ring
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unital
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            where each non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero"} zero
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
            has a two-sided
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
            and the multiplicative identity element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is not zero. Of course, one can require left and right inverse separately, as for an associative binary operation, if an element has both a left and a right inverse the two inverses are the same.
          %p
            Equivalently, skewfield is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+unital+algebra"} associative unital
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reciprocal+algebra"} reciprocal algebra
            where the multiplicative identity element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is not zero.
          %p
            Terminology “division ring” points to the fact that this is a unital ring in which any element can be divided by any nonzero element from the left and right, more precisely, the equations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a x = b
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y a = b
            have unique solutions iff
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\neq 0
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative
            skewfield is called a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %em field
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %em skewfield
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internally
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fields"} fields
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} Linear algebra
            is often understood in the generality of division rings, namely the usual notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix"} matrix
            of a linear map with respect to two bases and so on, and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+elimination"} Gauss elimination
            procedure, hold without changes for left or right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            over a division rings.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p Every finite skewfield is a field (Wedderburn’s little theorem).
          %h2#sources_of_examples Sources of examples
          %h3#quotients_of_domains Quotients of domains
          %p
            For every commutative domain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism#epiring"} epimorphism of rings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\to Q
            which is an injection and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Rings
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R/Rings
            and the field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is called the quotient field of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            For a noncommutative domain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            such an injective epimorphism of rings may not exist, or if it exists it is in general nonunique. For this reason many constructions of quotient skewfields are studied in the literature.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a left or right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ore+domain"} Ore domain
            then one can define the Ore quotient skewfield as the field of all left or right fractions with all nonzero denominators.
          %h3#wedderburnartin_theorem Wedderburn-Artin theorem
          %p
            An important appearance of division rings is via the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wedderburn-Artin+theorem"} Wedderburn-Artin theorem
          %h3#finite_dimensional_division_algebras Finite dimensional division algebras
          %p
            Any finite-dimensional algebra without zero divisors is a skew-field. Any division ring is a division algebra over its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/center"} center
          %p
            The most famous noncommutative example of a skewfield is the skewfield of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+theorem"} Frobenius theorem
            states that apart from the fields of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            and quaternions, there are no associative finite-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+algebras"} division algebras
            over the real numbers; and even if one includes nonassociative finite-dimensional division algebras one obtains only one more example (the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebra"} normed division algebra
            for more on this.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
